        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <h2 id="sec-1">1. Design Goals &amp; Overview</h2>

        <h3 id="sec-1-1">1.1 Design Goals</h3>
        <p>OmniBuf is designed around four core goals:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Stay Compatible with <code>.proto</code> File Semantics and Types:
            </strong>
            OmniBuf uses existing <code>.proto</code> definitions, field numbers (<code>1..N</code>
            ), scalar types, nested messages, repeated lists, and maps without requiring a new IDL,
            custom memory layouts, or manual alignment rules. Existing Protocol Buffers schemas and
            generated struct types can target the OmniBuf wire format directly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Faster Encoding and Decoding on Modern Hardware:</strong>
            Protocol Buffers (designed in 2001) relies on 7-bit LEB128 continuation loops (
            <code>0x80</code> branch per byte), two-pass <code>Size()</code> calculations for nested
            sub-messages, and byte-by-byte varint parsing when skipping fields. OmniBuf aligns the
            wire format with modern 64-bit little-endian CPUs:
            <ul>
              <li>
                Single-instruction trailing-zero count (<code>TZCNT</code> / <code>CTZ</code>)
                varints load <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, <code>4</code>, or <code>8</code> bytes
                in a single unaligned little-endian memory access.
              </li>
              <li>
                Single-pass forward encoding reserves and backpatches 2-byte length prefixes in
                place without a pre-pass.
              </li>
              <li>
                Explicit 4-bit wire types and outer container byte-length prefixes allow decoders to
                skip entire fields or nested containers in a single branchless <code>TZCNT</code>{' '}
                header hop without parsing inside strings, sub-messages, or arrays.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Schema-Less Value Decoding (with Optional Inline Field Names):</strong>
            In Protocol Buffers, Wire Type <code>2</code> (<code>LEN</code>) is overloaded for
            strings, raw bytes, nested messages, and packed repeated scalars, making it impossible
            to decode a packet without the <code>.proto</code> schema. OmniBuf uses explicit 4-bit
            wire types (<code>0..15</code>) so any decoder can losslessly inspect and decode every
            field's exact type and structure (<code>DecodeBlind</code>, including distinguishing
            unsigned <code>WireUint</code> / <code>WireUintArr</code> integers from ZigZag-encoded
            signed <code>WireSint</code> / <code>WireSintArr</code> integers, fixed-width float
            arrays, and arbitrary typed maps) with zero schema knowledge. When human-readable keys
            are desired on the wire, an optional inline schema sub-message (
            <strong>
              Field <code>#0</code>
            </strong>
            , <code>omnibuf.v1.SchemaMeta</code>) embeds field-number-keyed field names and nested
            child schemas at byte <code>0</code> of the root message (or any nested sub-message).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Advanced Wire Techniques with a Flat Stream Layout:</strong>
            Rather than requiring vtables, footer offset tables, relative pointer graphs, or arena
            allocators, OmniBuf achieves single-pass encoding, fast zero-copy unmarshaling, raw byte
            concatenation (<code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>), and rapid header-hop field/array/map
            traversal using a single contiguous stream of <code>[Tag][Value]</code> fields with 4
            integer widths and 16 explicit wire types.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h3 id="sec-1-2">1.2 Core Wire Primitives</h3>
        <p>
          To achieve these goals, OmniBuf replaces Protobuf's Tag-Length-Value (TLV) + LEB128 with
          four wire primitives:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Branchless Prefix-Varints (<code>TZCNT</code>):
            </strong>{' '}
            The trailing zero bit count (<code>TZCNT</code>) of the first byte determines the total
            byte width (<code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, <code>4</code>, or <code>9</code> bytes) in
            little-endian order, eliminating byte-by-byte continuation-bit loops.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Explicit 4-Bit Wire Types (<code>0..15</code>):
            </strong>{' '}
            Every field tag encodes <code>(FieldNumber &lt;&lt; 4) | WireType</code>, unambiguously
            distinguishing unsigned integers (<code>WireUint</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>),
            ZigZag-encoded signed integers (<code>WireSint</code>, <code>WireSintArr</code>),
            strings, bytes, sub-messages, string arrays, length-prefixed sub-message arrays, typed
            maps, fixed-width floats/ints (and packed arrays <code>WireFixedArr</code>), zero-byte
            booleans (<code>true</code>/<code>false</code> encoded directly in the tag), and a
            length-prefixed extension escape hatch (<code>WireExtended = 15</code>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Single-Pass Overlong Length Backpatching:</strong> Variable-length containers (
            <code>WireSubMsg</code>, <code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>,{' '}
            <code>WireSintArr</code>, <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>, <code>WireMap</code>) reserve a
            2-byte Prefix-Varint length slot (<code>TZCNT = 1</code>) before writing their body in a
            single forward pass and backpatch the 14-bit length upon completion, eliminating
            two-pass <code>Size()</code> pre-computation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Concat-Compatible Field Stream with Outer Container Lengths:</strong> Because
            every scalar width is encoded in <code>b[0]</code> via <code>TZCNT</code> and every
            container (<code>WireString</code>, <code>WireBytes</code>, <code>WireSubMsg</code>,{' '}
            <code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>, <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>,{' '}
            <code>WireMap</code>, <code>WireSintArr</code>, <code>WireFixedArr</code>,{' '}
            <code>WireExtended</code>) begins with its total <code>ByteLen</code>, a decoder can
            skip any field in a single <code>O(1)</code> arithmetic jump without parsing inside
            sub-messages or arrays, and concatenating two encoded OmniBuf messages (
            <code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>) produces a valid merged OmniBuf message.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <!-- SECTION 2 -->
        <h2 id="sec-2">2. Message Packet Layout &amp; Concatenation Semantics</h2>
        <p>
          Every OmniBuf root message (and every nested sub-message in <code>WireSubMsg</code> or{' '}
          <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>) is a contiguous sequence of encoded <code>[Tag][Value]</code>{' '}
          fields:
        </p>
        <pre><code> 0                                                                                N
 +--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+-----------------+
 | Field #0 (opt)     | Field #f_1         | Field #f_2         | ... Field #f_k  |
 | [Tag=0x0B][Schema] | [Tag][Value]       | [Tag][Value]       | [Tag][Value]    |
 +--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+-----------------+
 |&lt;---------------------------------- N bytes -----------------------------------&gt;|</code></pre>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Field Ordering &amp; Raw Message Concatenation (<code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>):
            </strong>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Encoders SHOULD emit fields in ascending order of <code>FieldNumber</code> for
                deterministic output.
              </li>
              <li>
                Decoders MUST accept fields in any order and MUST support duplicate field numbers so
                that concatenating two encoded messages (<code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>) or
                appending an updated <code>[Tag][Value]</code> to the end of an existing packet is
                valid and follows Protocol Buffers merge semantics:
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <strong>
                      Singular Scalar Fields (<code>WireUint</code>, <code>WireSint</code>,{' '}
                      <code>WireBoolFalse</code>, <code>WireBoolTrue</code>, <code>WireString</code>
                      , <code>WireBytes</code>, <code>WireFixed32</code>, <code>WireFixed64</code>):
                    </strong>{' '}
                    Last occurrence wins (<strong>"last write wins"</strong>).
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>
                      Repeated Fields (<code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>,{' '}
                      <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>, <code>WireSintArr</code>,{' '}
                      <code>WireFixedArr</code>):
                    </strong>{' '}
                    Elements from subsequent occurrences are appended in order to elements from
                    earlier occurrences.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>
                      Nested Sub-Messages (<code>WireSubMsg</code>):
                    </strong>{' '}
                    Subsequent occurrences of the same sub-message field are merged recursively into
                    the sub-message decoded from earlier occurrences.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>
                      Maps (<code>WireMap</code>):
                    </strong>{' '}
                    Entries from subsequent occurrences of the same map field are merged into the
                    map (with later keys overriding earlier keys).
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Zero-Byte Empty Message:</strong> An empty message with zero present fields is{' '}
            <code>0</code> bytes (<code>N = 0</code>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No Trailing Footer or Padding:</strong> The message ends immediately after the
            last byte of the final present field.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <!-- SECTION 3 -->
        <h2 id="sec-3">
          3. Prefix-Varint Encoding (<code>TZCNT</code>)
        </h2>
        <p>
          All variable-length unsigned integers (field tags, <code>WireUint</code> values,
          string/byte/container lengths, and array/map counts) are encoded as{' '}
          <strong>Prefix-Varints</strong>.
        </p>

        <h3 id="sec-3-1">3.1 Bit-Level Layout</h3>
        <p>
          The number of trailing zero bits (<code>TZCNT</code>) in Byte 0 (<code>b[0]</code>)
          specifies the total byte length of the integer (<code>1</code>, <code>2</code>,{' '}
          <code>4</code>, or <code>9</code> bytes). The remaining high bits of the little-endian
          word store the unsigned value:
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>TZCNT</th>
                <th>Byte 0</th>
                <th>Width</th>
                <th>Range</th>
                <th>Encode / Decode (Little-Endian)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>0</strong>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <code>vvvvvvv1</code>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <strong>1 B</strong> (7b)
                </td>
                <td>0 .. 127</td>
                <td>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Enc:</strong> <code>uint8(v &lt;&lt; 1) | 0x01</code>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Dec:</strong> <code>uint64(b[0] &gt;&gt; 1)</code>
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>1</strong>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <code>vvvvvv10</code>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <strong>2 B</strong> (14b)
                </td>
                <td>0 .. 16,383</td>
                <td>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Enc:</strong> <code>uint16(v &lt;&lt; 2) | 0x02</code>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Dec:</strong> <code>uint64(LE16(b[0:2]) &gt;&gt; 2)</code>
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>2</strong>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <code>vvvvv100</code>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <strong>4 B</strong> (29b)
                </td>
                <td>0 .. 536,870,911</td>
                <td>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Enc:</strong> <code>uint32(v &lt;&lt; 3) | 0x04</code>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Dec:</strong> <code>uint64(LE32(b[0:4]) &gt;&gt; 3)</code>
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>&gt;= 3</strong>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <code>00000000</code>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <strong>9 B</strong> (64b)
                </td>
                <td>
                  0 .. 2<sup>64</sup> - 1
                </td>
                <td>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Enc:</strong> <code>b[0] = 0x00</code>, <code>LE64(b[1:9]) = v</code>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Dec:</strong> <code>LE64(b[1:9])</code>
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <pre><code>1-Byte Prefix-Varint (TZCNT = 0, 7-bit value):
  Byte 0
  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
  | v6| v5| v4| v3| v2| v1| v0| 1 |
  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

2-Byte Prefix-Varint (TZCNT = 1, 14-bit value, Little-Endian):
  Byte 0 (LSB)                      Byte 1 (MSB)
  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+ +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
  | v5| v4| v3| v2| v1| v0| 1 | 0 | |v13|v12|v11|v10| v9| v8| v7| v6|
  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+ +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+

4-Byte Prefix-Varint (TZCNT = 2, 29-bit value, Little-Endian):
  Byte 0 (LSB)                      Byte 1          Byte 2          Byte 3 (MSB)
  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+ +-------------+ +-------------+ +-------------+
  | v4| v3| v2| v1| v0| 1 | 0 | 0 | |  v12 .. v5  | |  v20 .. v13 | |  v28 .. v21 |
  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+ +-------------+ +-------------+ +-------------+

9-Byte Prefix-Varint (Byte 0 == 0x00, 64-bit value, Little-Endian):
  Byte 0                            Bytes 1..8
  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+ +---------------------------------------------+
  | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |       uint64 little-endian (v63 .. v0)      |
  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+ +---------------------------------------------+</code></pre>

        <h3 id="sec-3-2">3.2 Prefix-Varint Binary &amp; Hex Examples</h3>

        <h4 id="sec-3-2-ex1">Example 1: v = 0 (1 Byte, TZCNT = 0)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Formula: <code>(0 &lt;&lt; 1) | 1 = 1</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Binary: <code>00000001</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Hex: <code>01</code>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="sec-3-2-ex2">Example 2: v = 127 (Maximum 1-Byte Value, TZCNT = 0)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Formula: <code>(127 &lt;&lt; 1) | 1 = 255</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Binary: <code>11111111</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Hex: <code>ff</code>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="sec-3-2-ex3">Example 3: v = 128 (Minimum Canonical 2-Byte Value, TZCNT = 1)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Formula: <code>uint16(128 &lt;&lt; 2) | 2 = 512 | 2 = 514 = 0x0202</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Little-Endian Bytes: <code>b[0] = 0x02</code> (<code>00000010</code>,{' '}
            <code>TZCNT = 1</code>), <code>b[1] = 0x02</code> (<code>00000010</code>)
          </li>
          <li>
            Hex: <code>02 02</code>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="sec-3-2-ex4">Example 4: v = 300 (2 Bytes, TZCNT = 1)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Binary of <code>300</code>: <code>00000001 00101100</code> (<code>0x012C</code>)
          </li>
          <li>
            Shift left by 2 bits and OR with <code>0b10</code> (<code>2</code>):{' '}
            <code>(300 &lt;&lt; 2) | 2 = 1200 | 2 = 1202 = 0x04B2</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Little-Endian Bytes: <code>b[0] = 0xB2</code> (<code>10110010</code>, low 2 bits are{' '}
            <code>10</code> &rarr; <code>TZCNT = 1</code>), <code>b[1] = 0x04</code> (
            <code>00000100</code>)
          </li>
          <li>
            Hex: <code>b2 04</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Decoding check:{' '}
            <code>LE16(b2 04) &gt;&gt; 2 = 0x04B2 &gt;&gt; 2 = 1202 &gt;&gt; 2 = 300</code>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="sec-3-2-ex5">Example 5: v = 16,384 (Minimum 4-Byte Value, TZCNT = 2)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Formula: <code>uint32(16384 &lt;&lt; 3) | 4 = 131072 | 4 = 131076 = 0x00020004</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Little-Endian Bytes: <code>b[0] = 0x04</code> (<code>00000100</code>,{' '}
            <code>TZCNT = 2</code>), <code>b[1] = 0x00</code>, <code>b[2] = 0x02</code>,{' '}
            <code>b[3] = 0x00</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            Hex: <code>04 00 02 00</code>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="sec-3-2-ex6">
          Example 6: v = 536,870,912 (2<sup>29</sup>, Minimum 9-Byte Value, TZCNT &gt;= 3)
        </h4>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Since <code>v &gt;= 2^29</code>, Byte 0 is <code>0x00</code> followed by{' '}
            <code>uint64(536870912) = 0x0000000020000000</code> in little-endian:
          </li>
          <li>
            Hex: <code>00 00 00 00 20 00 00 00 00</code>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="sec-3-3">3.3 Overlong 2-Byte Prefix-Varints (Single-Pass Length Backpatching)</h3>
        <p>
          Decoders MUST dispatch strictly on <code>TZCNT(b[0])</code> without rejecting non-minimal
          ("overlong") encodings. Specifically, a value <code>v &lt; 128</code> encoded using the
          2-byte <code>TZCNT = 1</code> formula (<code>uint16(v &lt;&lt; 2) | 2</code>) is valid on
          the wire.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Fast Path (<code>byteLen &lt;= 16,383</code>):
            </strong>{' '}
            When encoding a length-prefixed container (<code>WireSubMsg</code>,{' '}
            <code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>, <code>WireSintArr</code>,{' '}
            <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>, <code>WireMap</code>) in a single forward pass, the encoder
            reserves 2 bytes at <code>lenPos</code>, writes the container body directly into the
            output buffer, and backpatches the 2-byte length slot in place:
            <code>LE16(buf[lenPos : lenPos+2]) = (byteLen &lt;&lt; 2) | 2</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Large Container Expansion (<code>byteLen &gt; 16,383</code>):
            </strong>{' '}
            If a container exceeds 16,383 bytes, <code>BackpatchContainerLen</code> shifts the
            container body right by 2 bytes (for 4-byte Prefix-Varint up to 512 MB) or 7 bytes (for
            9-byte Prefix-Varint) and writes the wider Prefix-Varint length header. Because
            containers close from innermost to outermost, outer containers automatically include any
            expanded inner header in their byte length.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Example (<code>byteLen = 42</code> encoded as Overlong 2-Byte Prefix-Varint):
            </strong>
            <code>(42 &lt;&lt; 2) | 2 = 168 | 2 = 170 = 0x00AA</code> &rarr; Hex: <code>aa 00</code>
            . Decoder reads <code>TZCNT(0xAA) == 1</code> (2 bytes) and computes{' '}
            <code>0x00AA &gt;&gt; 2 = 42</code>.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <h2 id="sec-4">
          4. Signed Integers (<code>ZigZag</code> Encoding, <code>WireSint = 12</code>)
        </h2>
        <p>
          Signed integers (<code>int32</code>, <code>sint32</code>, <code>int64</code>,{' '}
          <code>sint64</code>) are mapped to unsigned <code>uint64</code> values via{' '}
          <strong>ZigZag encoding</strong> before being written as a <code>WireSint</code> (
          <code>12</code>) Prefix-Varint. Giving signed integers their own explicit 4-bit wire type
          (<code>WireSint = 12</code>, distinct from <code>WireUint = 0</code>) allows schema-less
          decoders (<code>DecodeBlind</code>) to losslessly ZigZag-decode signed integers back to
          negative or positive <code>int64</code> values directly from wire bytes without a{' '}
          <code>.proto</code> schema.
        </p>

