        <!-- SECTION 10 -->
        <h2 id="sec-10">10. Wire Validity &amp; Malformed Packet Rejection Rules</h2>
        <p>
          A conforming OmniBuf decoder MUST validate untrusted wire input and reject malformed or
          adversarial packets without panicking or reading out of bounds:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Empty Message:</strong> A <code>0</code>-byte slice (
            <code>len(packet) == 0</code>) is a valid empty message with zero present fields.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>
              <code>Field #0</code> Wire Type &amp; Structure:
            </strong>{' '}
            Any occurrence of <code>Field #0</code> MUST have <code>WireType == WireSubMsg</code> (
            <code>5</code>) and encode a valid <code>omnibuf.v1.SchemaMeta</code> sub-message (where{' '}
            <code>fields</code> is a <code>WireMap</code> with <code>MapType = 0x03</code> and{' '}
            <code>children</code> is a <code>WireMap</code> with <code>MapType = 0x05</code>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Defined 4-Bit Wire Types:</strong> Wire types <code>0..14</code> are core wire
            types and <code>WireExtended</code> (<code>15</code>) is the length-prefixed extension
            escape hatch. Any tag whose payload violates its wire type's structural rules MUST be
            rejected.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Payload &amp; Container Bounds:</strong>
            <ul>
              <li>
                No Prefix-Varint or fixed-width value (<code>WireFixed32</code>,{' '}
                <code>WireFixed64</code>) may read past <code>len(packet)</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                Length-prefixed fields (<code>WireString</code>, <code>WireBytes</code>,{' '}
                <code>WireSubMsg</code>, <code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>,{' '}
                <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>, <code>WireMap</code>, <code>WireSintArr</code>,{' '}
                <code>WireFixedArr</code>, <code>WireExtended</code>) MUST satisfy{' '}
                <code>byteLen &lt;= uint64(len(packet) - pos)</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>, and <code>WireSintArr</code>{' '}
                elements MUST end on an exact element boundary at <code>pos + byteLen</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireFixedArr</code> MUST satisfy <code>byteLen &gt;= 1</code>, with{' '}
                <code>ElemType == WireFixed32</code> (<code>10</code>) and{' '}
                <code>(byteLen - 1) % 4 == 0</code>, or <code>ElemType == WireFixed64</code> (
                <code>11</code>) and <code>(byteLen - 1) % 8 == 0</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireExtended</code> MUST satisfy <code>byteLen &gt;= 1</code> (at least 1 byte
                for <code>ExtType</code>); schema-less decoders MUST reject unrecognized{' '}
                <code>ExtType</code> values, while schema-aware decoders skip unknown fields with{' '}
                <code>WireExtended</code> in <code>O(1)</code> time.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireSubMsgArr</code> elements MUST each satisfy{' '}
                <code>elemLen &lt;= uint64(len(arrPayload) - pos)</code> and the final element MUST
                end exactly at <code>len(arrPayload)</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireMap</code> MUST satisfy <code>byteLen &gt;= 2</code>, contain a valid
                1-byte <code>MapType</code> header, satisfy{' '}
                <code>entryCount*2 &lt;= uint64(len(mapPayload) - pos)</code> before allocating
                entries (preventing OOM pre-allocation attacks), and the final entry MUST end
                exactly at <code>len(mapPayload)</code>. Duplicate keys follow "last write wins"
                semantics.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Resource &amp; Message Size Limits:</strong>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>
                  Maximum Nesting Depth (<code>MaxNestingDepth = 32</code>):
                </strong>{' '}
                By default, sub-message recursion deeper than 32 levels MUST be rejected.
                Implementations MAY provide an option to configure a custom recursion limit.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Message &amp; Container Byte-Size Limits:</strong> At the wire level,
                Prefix-Varints can represent container lengths up to{' '}
                <code>
                  2<sup>29</sup> - 1
                </code>{' '}
                bytes (<code>536,870,911</code> bytes, ~512 MiB) using a <code>4</code>-byte header
                and up to{' '}
                <code>
                  2<sup>64</sup> - 1
                </code>{' '}
                bytes using a <code>9</code>-byte header. To guard against memory exhaustion on
                untrusted inputs, implementations and transport layers MAY enforce configurable
                maximum message size limits:
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    RPC and framing layers MAY enforce a default per-message limit of{' '}
                    <code>4 MiB</code> (<code>4,194,304</code> bytes, matching gRPC's default
                    receive limit), and general-purpose decoders MAY enforce a default total message
                    limit of <code>64 MiB</code> (<code>67,108,864</code> bytes, matching Protocol
                    Buffers' default limit) unless overridden by the caller.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Implementations MAY reject individual container or string lengths exceeding{' '}
                    <code>536,870,911</code> bytes (<code>4</code>-byte Prefix-Varint maximum)
                    unless explicitly configured for &gt;512 MiB payloads.
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </main>
